module bindings.python.unbounded_concurrent_operations;

// A protocol handler that spawns one asyncio task per incoming message and
// files it into an instance collection keyed by message data holds every
// operation live for as long as the peer keeps it open: a subscription
// task never finishes until the peer says so, so a client that keeps
// sending new operation ids grows the registry and its task set without
// limit — one connection exhausts the process (CWE-400). asyncio's own
// documentation names the bounds for exactly this shape: compare the
// collection against a limit, or admit operations through a semaphore.
// The anchor is structural and spelling-neutral: a create_task /
// ensure_future spawn, an assignment into a keyed target, an instance
// collection subscripted in the same function, and the message
// destructuring that says the entries are per-message rather than
// once-per-connection bookkeeping — the common spellings of the incoming
// object as a subscript base. The negations are the language idioms for
// a count bound, not any one library's parameter name: a len() call
// anywhere in the handler (the size comparison a cap spells) or an
// acquire() call (semaphore- or pool-gated admission, the constructor
// living in another function) suppresses, so a limit spelled with any
// other name still clears the finding. Residuals, all recall- or
// one-directional: a cap kept as an explicit counter rather than a len()
// comparison stays flagged; an `async with semaphore:` admission emits no
// call token and stays flagged; and the assign and index clauses are
// separate existentials, so the keyed write and the instance collection
// need not be the same object. Function-local throughout: a len() in a
// sibling method does not suppress.
binding unboundedConcurrentOperationRegistration {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "python"
    and node.context.call in ["create_task", "ensure_future"]
    and node.context.assign contains "[]="
    and node.context.indexBase startsWith "self."
    and node.context.indexBase in ["message", "msg", "payload", "data", "event", "frame", "packet", "envelope", "request", "body"]
    and not (node.context.call in ["len", "acquire"])
  emit issue code.UnboundedResourceOperation at node
}
